{"id":"low-bid-then-growth","title":"Low bid, then growth","question":"Do construction contracts won well under the engineer's estimate, or by a hair, later grow past the estimate?","finding":"54 of 98 bid sheets on disk could be matched to a contract in the table, and 42 of those began at least a year ago. Their ceilings now stand a median +2.3% against the winning bid; 2 of the 54 have grown by a quarter or more. Winners that bid 10% or more under the engineer's estimate: 14, median growth since +2.8%, 0 now above the estimate; the other 27 with an estimate: median +1.3%. Won by 2% or less over the next bidder: 6, median growth +5.9%, against +1.6% for the 24 won by more. The largest rise: Bay Area Lightworks Inc, PW Cntrt No.36 Trffc Sgnl Mod (Public Works, bid $3.4 million in 2023, ceiling now $6.3 million, +82%). 8 of the matched contracts also appear in commission items that modify or close them out: Bay Area Lightworks Inc, PW Cntrt No.36 Trffc Sgnl Mod (bid $3.4 million, $6.5 million in the latest item, +90%; reason printed: work the owner added or changed); West Coast Arborists Inc, PW GAN G21 TREE SRVCS (bid $2.6 million, $3.8 million in the latest item, +50%); Buhler Commercial, PW 911 CALL CNTR RENO (bid $3.4 million, $4.1 million in the latest item, +20%). A rank test puts the chance of the low-bid group ranking that high with no real difference at 55%, and of the narrow-margin group at 21%. Neither gap is beyond chance on these few sheets, which are also too recent for most contracts to have finished.","how":"Each published bid tabulation (Public Works Commission 2022 to 2026; Port and Municipal Transportation Agency staff reports from 2015) is matched to a construction contract in the Supplier Contracts table held by one of its bidders, begun within 400 days after the bid date, in the same department, where the titles share a number and another word, or three distinctive words, or one distinctive word and two common ones, or the ceiling equals the bid to the dollar (the table shortens titles, so words are compared with their vowels dropped). The winner is the bidder that holds the contract. Where the same contract appears in a commission's modification or close-out items (matched by the City contract number, or the department's own number in the table title), the total, the days added and the reasons printed there are shown beside it; a total in an agenda item can include contingency the commission authorized but the contractor has not been paid. Growth is the present ceiling (every row of the contract added up) over that bidder's total bid; for Port sheets that print a base bid and alternates on one line the largest figure is taken as the total. \"Under the estimate\" is the winning bid against the engineer's estimate on the sheet; \"margin\" is the second-lowest bid over the lowest, shown where the lowest bidder won.","limits":"Change orders for conditions found underground, and work the owner adds, are an ordinary part of construction; the Budget and Legislative Analyst found change orders added 8% to first contract values across five departments. A ceiling can also rise because the City added a second phase or more streets to the same contract, which is new scope, not an overrun. Most sheets on disk are from 2022 or later, so many contracts are unfinished and their final cost is unknown. The match is by wording and can pair a sheet with the wrong contract; the row links to the sheet so the two can be compared. A bid amount read from a scanned table can be wrong. What would settle a row: the change-order log for that contract.","tier":"REDFLAG","columns":[{"k":"winner","t":"Won by","type":"org"},{"k":"agency","t":"Department","type":"text"},{"k":"contract","t":"Contract","type":"text"},{"k":"date","t":"Bids opened","type":"date"},{"k":"bidders","t":"Bidders","type":"int"},{"k":"bid","t":"Winning bid","type":"usd"},{"k":"estimate","t":"Engineer's estimate","type":"usd"},{"k":"underEstimate","t":"Bid against th